{"subjectId":"javascript","id":"type-coercion","title":"Type Coercion & == vs ===","group":"Types, Values & Operators","order":6,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">JavaScript silently converts values when an operator expects another type. Learn the three conversions (to string, number, boolean) and <code>==</code> stops being magic.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Type coercion</strong> — automatic (implicit) conversion of a value from one type to another by an operator — as opposed to <em>explicit</em> conversion with <code>String(x)</code>, <code>Number(x)</code>, <code>Boolean(x)</code>.</p></div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-hand-finger\"></i>Explicit conversion</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log(String(123), String(null), String([1, 2]), String({}));\nconsole.log(Number(\"42\"), Number(\"\"), Number(\"  7  \"), Number(\"4px\"));\nconsole.log(Number(true), Number(null), Number(undefined), Number([]), Number([5]));\nconsole.log(parseInt(\"4px\"), parseFloat(\"3.5em\"), parseInt(\"0x1F\"), parseInt(\"101\", 2));\nconsole.log(Boolean(\"\"), Boolean(\"0\"), Boolean([]), Boolean(0));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>123 null 1,2 [object Object]\n42 0 7 NaN\n1 0 NaN 0 5\n4 3.5 31 5\nfalse true true false</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Value</th><th><code>Number(v)</code></th><th><code>String(v)</code></th><th><code>Boolean(v)</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>undefined</code></td><td><code>NaN</code></td><td><code>\"undefined\"</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>null</code></td><td><code>0</code></td><td><code>\"null\"</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>true</code> / <code>false</code></td><td><code>1</code> / <code>0</code></td><td><code>\"true\"</code> / <code>\"false\"</code></td><td>same</td></tr><tr><td><code>\"\"</code></td><td><code>0</code></td><td><code>\"\"</code></td><td><code>false</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>\"12abc\"</code></td><td><code>NaN</code></td><td>same</td><td><code>true</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>[]</code></td><td><code>0</code></td><td><code>\"\"</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr><tr><td><code>{}</code></td><td><code>NaN</code></td><td><code>\"[object Object]\"</code></td><td><code>true</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-math-symbols\"></i>Implicit coercion by operators</h3>\n<ul><li><code>+</code> with <strong>any string</strong> operand → string concatenation. Otherwise numeric addition.</li><li><code>- * / % **</code> and unary <code>+</code> → always numeric.</li><li><code>if</code>, <code>!</code>, <code>&amp;&amp;</code>, <code>||</code>, <code>? :</code> → boolean (truthiness).</li><li>Objects are first turned into primitives via <code>Symbol.toPrimitive</code>, then <code>valueOf()</code>, then <code>toString()</code>.</li></ul>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log(1 + \"2\", \"3\" - 1, \"3\" * \"4\", +\"5\", +\"\");\nconsole.log(true + 1, null + 1, undefined + 1);\nconsole.log([] + [], [] + {}, [1, 2] + [3]);\nconsole.log(1 + 2 + \"3\", \"1\" + 2 + 3);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>12 2 12 5 0\n2 1 NaN\n [object Object] 1,23\n33 123</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Evaluation is left to right: <code>1 + 2 + \"3\"</code> is <code>3 + \"3\"</code> → <code>\"33\"</code>, while <code>\"1\" + 2 + 3</code> is <code>\"12\" + 3</code> → <code>\"123\"</code>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i><code>==</code> (loose) vs <code>===</code> (strict)</h3>\n<div class=\"grid\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal-double\"></i><code>===</code> strict equality</h3><p>No conversion: different types → <code>false</code>. Objects: same reference. The only surprise: <code>NaN !== NaN</code>.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-equal\"></i><code>==</code> loose equality</h3><p>Converts first: <code>null == undefined</code> (and nothing else), otherwise booleans → numbers, strings → numbers, objects → primitives.</p></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log(0 == \"\", 0 == \"0\", \"\" == \"0\");\nconsole.log(false == \"0\", false == [], null == 0, null == undefined);\nconsole.log([1] == 1, [1, 2] == \"1,2\", NaN == NaN);\nconsole.log(0 === \"\", null === undefined, 1 === 1.0);</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>true true false\ntrue true false true\ntrue true false\nfalse false true</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><code>==</code> is not transitive: <code>0 == \"\"</code> and <code>0 == \"0\"</code> but <code>\"\" != \"0\"</code>. Always use <code>===</code> — except <code>x == null</code>, a handy check for <em>null or undefined</em>.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-fingerprint\"></i><code>Object.is</code> — SameValue</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>console.log(NaN === NaN, Object.is(NaN, NaN));\nconsole.log(0 === -0, Object.is(0, -0));\nconsole.log([NaN].includes(NaN), [NaN].indexOf(NaN));</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>false true\ntrue false\ntrue -1</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Algorithm</th><th>Used by</th><th><code>NaN</code> vs <code>NaN</code></th><th><code>0</code> vs <code>-0</code></th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Strict (<code>===</code>)</td><td><code>===</code>, <code>indexOf</code>, <code>switch</code></td><td>not equal</td><td>equal</td></tr><tr><td>SameValueZero</td><td><code>includes</code>, <code>Map</code>/<code>Set</code> keys</td><td>equal</td><td>equal</td></tr><tr><td>SameValue</td><td><code>Object.is</code></td><td>equal</td><td>not equal</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Objects to primitives</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const price = {\n  valueOf() { return 42; },\n  toString() { return \"$42\"; },\n};\nconsole.log(price + 1, `${price}`, price &gt; 40);\n\nconst temp = {\n  [Symbol.toPrimitive](hint) {\n    return hint === \"number\" ? 21 : hint === \"string\" ? \"21°C\" : \"default\";\n  },\n};\nconsole.log(+temp, `${temp}`, temp + \"\");</code></pre></figure><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>43 $42 true\n21 21°C default</code></pre></figure>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is the difference between == and ===?</summary><div><p><code>===</code> compares without type conversion (and objects by identity). <code>==</code> first coerces operands to a common type using the abstract equality rules — <code>null == undefined</code>, booleans and strings become numbers, objects become primitives. Prefer <code>===</code>; <code>x == null</code> is the common exception.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What does [] + {} evaluate to?</summary><div><p><code>\"[object Object]\"</code>. Both are converted to primitives: <code>[]</code> → <code>\"\"</code>, <code>{}</code> → <code>\"[object Object]\"</code>, and <code>+</code> with a string concatenates. (Typed at the start of a line in a console, <code>{}</code> may be parsed as an empty block, which is why <code>{} + []</code> can show <code>0</code>.)</p></div></details></section>"}